format: meaning/draft-1
id: requirements
name: Requirements
description: >-
  What a change is for: requirements, use cases, acceptance criteria,
  stakeholders, business rules and the words they depend on.
license: CC0-1.0
concepts:
  - id: stakeholder
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Stakeholder
    synonyms:
      en: [interested party]
    description: >-
      A person, a group, or an organization with an interest in a change, a
      need, or a solution. BABOK includes groups. A group that is a team is
      recorded as the stakeholder's team, and an organization as the
      stakeholder's organization. A stakeholder is not a kind of person,
      because the interested party may be a team or an organization. The
      user named in a user story may be a stakeholder and may not be the
      only one. An employee may be a stakeholder and need not be.

  - id: stakeholder-person
    kind: attribute
    of: stakeholder
    labels:
      en: Stakeholder person
    description: >-
      The person who is this stakeholder, when the interested party is a
      person.
    values-of: person

  - id: stakeholder-organization
    kind: attribute
    of: stakeholder
    labels:
      en: Stakeholder organization
    description: >-
      The organization that is this stakeholder, when the interested party
      is an organization.
    values-of: organization

  - id: stakeholder-team
    kind: attribute
    of: stakeholder
    labels:
      en: Stakeholder team
    description: >-
      The team that is this stakeholder, when the interested party is a
      group rather than one person or a whole organization.
    values-of: team

  - id: requirement
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Requirement
    synonyms:
      en: [requirement statement]
    description: >-
      A usable representation of a need. The classes are a business
      requirement, which states why a change has been initiated, a
      stakeholder requirement, which states what a stakeholder needs so that
      can be achieved, a solution requirement, which states a capability or
      quality of the solution as a functional or non-functional requirement,
      and a transition requirement, which is needed only while moving to the
      new state. A user story is a delivery work item whose text often
      represents a stakeholder need. The story and this requirement are
      linked when both are recorded, and they are not the same record. A use
      case narrates an interaction and is not one of these classes. An
      acceptance criterion is a condition for accepting a story, a
      requirement, or a service. Work that delivers the requirement is a
      work item, linked from the requirement.

  - id: requirement-priority
    kind: attribute
    of: requirement
    labels:
      en: Requirement priority
    description: >-
      How important this requirement is relative to others. One scheme is
      MoSCoW, which sorts requirements into must, should, could and won't.
      That is a priority classification. It does not grade the severity of a
      running failure or of a defect. A rank or another local scheme uses
      this same attribute. The scheme is not fixed here, and priority is not
      status.

  - id: requirement-source
    kind: attribute
    of: requirement
    labels:
      en: Requirement source
    description: >-
      A stakeholder who raised or owns this requirement. The source is not
      the user in every user story written from it.
    values-of: stakeholder

  - id: requirement-status
    kind: attribute
    of: requirement
    labels:
      en: Requirement status
    description: >-
      Where this requirement sits between being proposed and being satisfied
      or dropped. The state names belong to the practice. Status is not
      priority.

  - id: requirement-work-item
    kind: attribute
    of: requirement
    labels:
      en: Requirement work item
    synonyms:
      en: [traced work item]
    description: >-
      A work item that delivers or checks this requirement. The link connects
      the recorded need to the delivery work. The work item does not replace
      the requirement, and a requirement can be met by more than one work
      item.
    values-of: work-item

  - id: requirement-business-rule
    kind: attribute
    of: requirement
    labels:
      en: Requirement business rule
    description: >-
      A business rule this requirement is meant to uphold. The rule can
      outlive the requirement, and the requirement is not the rule.
    values-of: business-rule

  - id: business-requirement
    kind: entity
    extends: requirement
    labels:
      en: Business requirement
    description: >-
      A requirement that states the goals, objectives, and outcomes for why
      a change has been initiated. It can apply to the whole organization, a
      business area, or one initiative. It is not a stakeholder requirement,
      which states what a stakeholder needs so those outcomes can be
      achieved, and it is not a functional requirement. It is not a user
      story. A story may represent part of the need and remains a work item.

  - id: stakeholder-requirement
    kind: entity
    extends: requirement
    labels:
      en: Stakeholder requirement
    description: >-
      A requirement that states what a stakeholder needs so the business
      requirements can be achieved. It bridges business requirements and
      solution requirements. It is not a business requirement and not a
      solution requirement. A user story often represents one of these needs
      and remains a work item, linked from the requirement when both are
      recorded.

  - id: solution-requirement
    kind: entity
    extends: requirement
    labels:
      en: Solution requirement
    description: >-
      A requirement that states a capability or a quality the solution must
      have to meet the stakeholder requirements, in enough detail to build
      and implement the solution. A functional requirement and a
      non-functional requirement are kinds of solution requirement. It is
      not a business requirement and not a transition requirement.

  - id: functional-requirement
    kind: entity
    extends: solution-requirement
    labels:
      en: Functional requirement
    description: >-
      A solution requirement that states a capability the solution must
      have: the behavior and information the solution will manage. It is not
      a non-functional requirement, which states a condition or a quality
      such as speed, security, or availability. It is not a business rule. A
      rule constrains a decision whether or not this solution is built. It
      is not a use case. A use case narrates the steps of an interaction
      that may satisfy several functions.

  - id: non-functional-requirement
    kind: entity
    extends: solution-requirement
    labels:
      en: Non-functional requirement
    synonyms:
      en: [nonfunctional requirement, quality of service requirement, NFR]
    description: >-
      A solution requirement that states a condition or quality of the
      solution rather than a function a user invokes: how fast it is, how it
      resists misuse, how available it is, how usable it is. BABOK also
      calls these quality of service requirements. Availability stated as a
      need is this kind of requirement. The measured availability of a
      running service is a different concept, and so is a service level
      objective, which is a target on a measurement. An objective may be how
      a quality requirement is checked in production. The objective is not
      the requirement.

  - id: transition-requirement
    kind: entity
    extends: requirement
    labels:
      en: Transition requirement
    description: >-
      A requirement that states a capability or condition needed only to
      move from the current state to the future state, such as converting
      data, training people, or keeping work going during the move. It is
      not needed once the change is complete. It is not a functional
      requirement of the future solution.

  - id: use-case
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Use case
    synonyms:
      en: [use-case]
    description: >-
      A description of how an actor reaches a goal by interacting with a
      system, including the main sequence and the alternatives. The actor
      may be a person, an organization, or another system. A use case is not
      a user story. The story is one backlog increment, while the use case
      is the interaction and can span more than one increment. It is not one
      of the requirement classes, though it may be written to show how
      requirements are met. It is not a business process. A business process
      is how the organization does a piece of work, whether or not that work
      is one session with a system.

  - id: use-case-actor
    kind: attribute
    of: use-case
    labels:
      en: Use case actor
    synonyms:
      en: [actor]
    description: >-
      The name of an actor in the use case. The actor may be a person, an
      organization, or another system, so this attribute is the name in the
      narrative rather than a reference to one kind of party. An actor need
      not be a stakeholder, because another system can be an actor, and need
      not be the only stakeholder of the requirements the use case touches.

  - id: use-case-requirement
    kind: attribute
    of: use-case
    labels:
      en: Use case requirement
    description: >-
      A requirement this use case shows being met. The use case can touch
      several requirements, and a requirement can be met without a use case
      being written.
    values-of: requirement

  - id: acceptance-criterion
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Acceptance criterion
    synonyms:
      en: [acceptance criteria]
    description: >-
      A condition that must be met before a user story, a requirement, or a
      service is accepted. ITIL states acceptance criteria for a service or
      a service component. Business analysis uses them for the minimum a
      solution must meet to be acceptable to key stakeholders, and delivery
      teams attach them to a user story. It is not the story text, not the
      requirement, and not a flow inside a use case. It is not an evaluation
      criterion, which ranks alternative solutions. One story or requirement
      can have several criteria. The plural acceptance criteria is the same
      concept.

  - id: acceptance-criterion-user-story
    kind: attribute
    of: acceptance-criterion
    labels:
      en: Acceptance criterion user story
    description:       The user story this condition is a check for, when it is attached to a
      story.
    values-of: user-story

  - id: acceptance-criterion-requirement
    kind: attribute
    of: acceptance-criterion
    labels:
      en: Acceptance criterion requirement
    description: >-
      A requirement this condition is a check for, when it is attached to a
      requirement rather than only to a story.
    values-of: requirement

  - id: acceptance-criterion-service
    kind: attribute
    of: acceptance-criterion
    labels:
      en: Acceptance criterion service
    description: >-
      A service this condition must be met for, when the criterion is stated
      for the service rather than for one story.
    values-of: service

  - id: business-rule
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Business rule
    description: >-
      A specific, practicable, testable directive under the control of the
      business, which guides behavior or a decision. An example is a limit
      above which a second person must approve. It holds whether or not a
      particular solution is built. It is not a requirement. A requirement is
      a usable representation of a need a solution is expected to meet,
      though a requirement may exist so that a solution upholds a rule. It
      is not an acceptance criterion, which checks a story, a requirement, or
      a service.

  - id: business-process
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Business process
    synonyms:
      en: [business workflow]
    description: >-
      A repeatable way the organization carries out a piece of its work: a
      sequence of steps with a reason to start and a result. It is not a use
      case, which is one actor's interaction with a system, and not a
      project, which is a temporary undertaking. Software can support a
      process; the process is not a software component.

  - id: process-step
    kind: entity
    of: business-process
    labels:
      en: Process step
    description: >-
      One part of a business process: a single action or decision in that
      sequence. The step is not the whole process and not a work item on a
      delivery backlog.

  - id: process-step-business-process
    kind: attribute
    of: process-step
    labels:
      en: Process step business process
    description: The business process this step is part of.
    values-of: business-process

  - id: glossary-term
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Glossary term
    synonyms:
      en: [defined term]
    description: >-
      A word with a stated meaning for a business domain, so that
      requirements, rules and processes use it in that meaning. It is not a
      requirement and not a business rule. The term defines a word; a rule
      constrains a decision.

  - id: kpi
    kind: entity
    labels:
      en: Key performance indicator
    synonyms:
      en: [KPI]
    description: >-
      A metric an organization has chosen to judge success in meeting an
      objective. Which formula it uses is part of that choice, so a key
      performance indicator is recorded as an entity and not as one measure
      with a formula fixed here. It is not a service level indicator. A
      service level indicator measures the behavior of a service. A key
      performance indicator judges a business result. An organization can
      watch either, and choosing one does not make it the other.

  - id: kpi-business-process
    kind: attribute
    of: kpi
    labels:
      en: Key performance indicator business process
    description: >-
      A business process whose performance this indicator is used to judge,
      when the indicator is about a process.
    values-of: business-process
